#set par(justify: true)

= Self-improving automatic procedural 3D modeling workflow for interactive applications

== Background

3D modeling is normally an artist's job using integrated UI-heavy software #link("https://www.blender.org/")[Blender].
Their workflow is more similar to a traditional artist (painter #link("https://www.adobe.com/products/substance3d/features.html")[Substance 3D] / sculptor #link("https://www.blender.org/features/sculpting/")[Blender sculpting]) and relies on repeated use of smaller, simpler controls.
An artist requires taste, intuition, and experience to know how to operate tools to achieve the overarching goal.
Programmers lacking those skills are lost in the possibility space, and might be put off by the repetitive nature and lack of a unique general solution to accomplish the design.
However, the creation of many scenes is impractical to realize solely through those direct tools. It requires more abstract procedural tools (nodes, scripting) or even dedicated subprograms, often running complex processes or physical simulations.

#link("https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/geometry_nodes/introduction.html")[Geometry Nodes]

#link("https://openscad.org")[CAD scripting]

#link("https://www.sidefx.com/products/whats-new-in-h22/world-building")[Dedicated subprograms]

There is always a tension in tool design between efficiency and flexibility.

== Opportunity

Besides artistic clicking or abstract programming, there is now a third workflow emerging: AI agents can independently operate the software needed to iterate on a design, with a human tester providing feedback and ideas for improvement.
While LLMs can operate traditional software suites like Blender, they open a new method: programming the art directly in a general-purpose language.
Besides the conversation workflow for a given project, humans cooperate to develop examples and tools that the AI can use in turn.

Claude using ThreeJS: #link("https://claude.ai/artifact/3bhygX763cPdpcHQYmfrPk")[artifact]

Those examples and tools can themselves have been generated by prompting an LLM: #link("https://github.com/CK42BB/procedural-grass-threejs/blob/main/SKILL.md")[procedural-grass-threejs skill]

(And thus rely on the LLM's experience gained by reading previous projects.)

== Drawback

The main drawback of this approach is that the AI produces a lot of unfamiliar low-level code (slop), which is both hard to review and prompt.
It also wastes a lot of tokens on reasoning and iteration.
This is because the LLM has to connect two extremes: from a general-purpose programming language to the product (3D primitives like mesh triangle soup or textures).
While there is much existing software that helps build bridges along the way, the challenge is figuring out how to make those programs work together.
Humans can improve the approach by prompting specific solutions after reviewing the inner mechanisms, and by introducing tests that constrain and verify the intermediary results.
These specific fixes have to be repeated for every project and are hard to exploit automatically across projects simply by accumulating a collection of examples (sometimes generalized as skills), and through the LLM learning a little technique from a published training set that grows over time.
Especially since the generated code is AI slop, a pyramid of patchworks accumulates and becomes more and more unmanageable for human control.

== Solution

The long path from prompt to app has to be broken by a clean, elegant language connecting LLMs and humans at this intermediary level, and allowing them to compose powerful elements.
This language should be familiar and compact enough to support reasoning for both the LLM and the human helping it design and learn this language.
Its purpose need only be general enough to justify the combined effort, while greater specificity helps simplify its construction.
To justify the effort of being constrained by such a node, it is important to grow the software's usability by incorporating a range of useful concrete tools and enabling high-level constructs.
This philosophy can be applied to many domains at many abstraction levels.
Common infrastructure can be shared between implementations placed at sweet spots in the abstraction-by-domain meaning space.

== Instance

Our application domain here is 3D modeling.
The abstraction level is a declarative language specifying a graph of geometrical operations on a boundary representation.
Since the scope of the project implies a large-scale effort, implementation requires a collective intelligence network.

== Current state

The closest platform I could find is #link("https://mucad.org")[μcad].
The main challenge is integrating all the tools needed for general 3D modeling beyond CAD: #link("https://github.com/mucad-lang/mucad/compare/main...Matthias-Fauconneau:mucad:main")[μcad changes] (GPT/Codex assisted).
For example, a mangrove has nothing to do with a typical CAD part: #link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangrove")[mangrove] vs. #link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer-aided_design")[computer-aided design].

1. Define the scene to model (assisted): parametric mangrove forest cluster of trees with varied scales. Each tree should have a leaning, multi-segment trunk; arching stilt prop roots of varying heights and radial spreads; curved upper branches; and a green canopy with dark brown bark.
2. Generate μcad code with the help of instructions and the μcad compiler (assisted):
   - #link("https://codeberg.org/Matthias_Fauconneau/mucad/raw/commit/3ec1b7e62e47d3519d7c7e6843dccaab5603e4be/examples/mangrove.mu")[mangrove.mu]
   - #link("https://gist.github.com/Matthias-Fauconneau/368a6f88843b32c7e609737b1ec72955")[instructions]
   - Any human help required at this step can generalize across prompts by folding hints into the instructions.
3. μcad generates the mesh and can export glTF. #link("https://github.com/Matthias-Fauconneau/glb-opt")[glb-opt] simplifies the mesh for performance and file size.
4. Review the object: #link("https://github.com/Matthias-Fauconneau/glbviewer")[glbviewer] and #link("https://codeberg.org/attachments/d2653cea-723c-4516-9d41-6754435f8c2a")[rendered result].

   > What do you think you could improve? Do you miss any tool to make it easier to generate a more realistic mangrove tree?
   > — Gemini, #link("https://gist.github.com/Matthias-Fauconneau/7da254e4b8ae182f7bb8d3df14679fbf")[feedback]

   Any human help can improve the tools (Codex), edit the instructions and prompt, and retry until convergence.

